==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{q|verb}} {{IPA|en|/ˌəʊvə(ɹ)ˈdɹaɪv/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-overdrive (verb).wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{q|noun}} {{IPA|en|/ˈəʊvə(ɹ)ˌdɹaɪv/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-overdrive (noun).wav|a=Southern England}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|overdriven}}, from {{inh|en|ang|oferdrīfan}}, equivalent to {{af|en|over-|drive}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|uurdrieuwe|t=to overdo, overstate}}, {{cog|nl|overdrijven|t=to exaggerate}}, {{cog|nds-de|overdrieven}}, {{m|nds-de|överdrieven|t=to overdo, exaggerate}}, {{cog|de|übertreiben|t=to overdo, exaggerate}}, {{cog|no|overdrive|t=to exaggerate}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|overdrives|overdriving|overdrove|overdriven}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[drive]] too hard, or far, or beyond strength.

===Etymology 2===
From {{af|en|over-|drive}}, originally referring to [[gear ratio]]s over 1:1<!--numerically less than 1 but idiomatically higher ratio-->.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|dated|automotive}} A [[gear]], on an automobile, higher than the traditionally normal [[top gear]], mainly to allow better [[fuel economy]] at highway speeds. {{ngd|In the 21st century, such gears are no longer viewed as "extra" and thus are often not called by this name anymore.}}
#: {{syn|en|q=initialisms|OD|O/D|O.D.}}
#: {{cot|en|fourth gear|fifth gear|<!--variable semantic relations: the one under it or another name for it, depending on the transmission at hand--><!--|sixth gear|seventh gear-->}}
#: {{ux|en|In the 1970s, many transmission designs were revised to add an '''overdrive''' gear.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=Popular Mechanics|page=201|year=1981|month=October|volume=156|number=4|passage=Cruising in '''overdrive''' at legal highway speed keeps rpm right at 1800, depending on rear-axle ratio.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|figurative}} A state of heightened activity.
#: {{ux|en|The campaign has been in '''overdrive''' since the recent announcement.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The ground beneath her feet|page=78|author=w:Salman Rushdie|year=2000|passage=It is true, though it's got nothing to do with me, that the building boom that created the Bombay of my childhood went into '''overdrive''' in the years before my birth}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Mike Isaac|title=‘Operating With Increased Intensity’: Zuckerberg Leads Meta Into Next Phase|work=The New York Times|date=2022-07-26|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/26/technology/zuckerberg-meta-facebook-earnings.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=So, Mr. Zuckerberg has kicked his company into '''overdrive''' with a strong message: It’s time to do more with less.}}
# {{senseid|en|angel gear, euphemistically}} {{lb|en|euphemism|slang}} [[freewheel#Verb|Freewheeling]] (driving a vehicle with the [[transmission]] in [[neutral#Noun|neutral]]) on [[downhill]] stretches to achieve higher [[fuel economy]] and/or [[speed]] than otherwise. {{ngd|In [[colloquial]] registers, ethnically bigoted variants are common, such as ''Jewish overdrive'' (alluding to the stereotype of miserly Jews) or ''Mexican overdrive'' or ''Polish overdrive'' (alluding to stereotypes of foolishness and poverty).}}
#: {{syn|en|''see at'' [[angel gear]]}}
#: {{ux|en|Of course '''overdrive''' is forbidden for dump trucks in the quarry because it is unsafe, but unscrupulous workers were found to be using it because of a perverse incentive involving tonnage piece rates.}}
# {{lb|en|engineering|mechanical}} A [[gear ratio]] higher than 1:1<!--numerically less than 1 but idiomatically higher ratio-->: one in which a single revolution of the driving element corresponds to more than one revolution of the driven element.

